// T4 gate: does a slice that ARRIVED from a shim free the elements it owns?
//
// PR #274 gave a slice its elements, but only the ones the program STORED --
// a literal's elements, or an append's arguments. A slice that arrives fully
// populated from a call stores nothing, so it owned nothing and its parts
// leaked. `fields := strings.Split(req, ",")` is that shape, and it is the
// daemon's first statement.
//
// The proof lives in the table, not here: strings.Split carries
// returns_owned_elems = 1 in src/types/shim_signatures.c, audited against
// goo_strings_split, which goo_allocs the goo_string_t array and then builds
// every part with goo_string_new_with_length / goo_string_new -- both COPY.
// So no element points into the argument, and the returned slice is their
// single owner.
//
// Measured on bench/daemon/daemon.goo at 2,000 requests: 1,225,982 bytes lost
// before, 952,000 after. 273,982 bytes, the largest single item left after
// #274, and exactly the figure .handoff.md projected.
//
// WHY THE BYTES ARE READ AND NOT len(). The first draft of #274's probe
// checked len(owned), which loads field 1 of the {i8*, i64} string and never
// dereferences the buffer -- it passed against the exact mutant it existed to
// catch. The concatenation below memcpys out of both element buffers, so a
// premature release shows up as an Invalid read.
//
// TWO ELEMENTS AND ONE CONCAT, deliberately. `a + b + c` would build an
// unbound intermediate, and nothing releases a temporary no local names, so
// the probe would leak in BOTH builds and the ON assertion could not be
// "0 bytes".
package main

import "strings"

var sink int

func build() int {
	fields := strings.Split("alpha,beta", ",")
	// Reads the BYTES of both elements: goo_string_concat memcpys out of each
	// buffer. `joined` is bound once to a `+`, so it is released too.
	joined := fields[0] + fields[1]
	return len(joined)
}

func main() {
	total := 0
	for i := 0; i < 10000; i++ {
		total = total + build()
	}
	sink = total
	// Printed so the gate can diff this against the GOO_ARC_RELEASE=0 control.
	// PR #264 measured a release that valgrind called clean while it silently
	// corrupted eight bytes of a live string, so a valgrind-only check has no
	// teeth against a whole class of defect.
	println(total)
}
